module std.math

// std.math (T461, SPEC-41 AXS-FLT-060..064): the deterministic elementary and transcendental library of f32 and
// f64, written in Axiom over the five float intrinsics of std.core.float (ADR-0049). There is no libm, no CRT and
// no hardware transcendental instruction: every function produces the same bits in the MIR interpreter and in
// native code, on every host (INV-FLT-004).
//
// Naming: the f64 functions have the plain names (`std.math.sin(x)`), the f32 ones the suffix `_f32`
// (`std.math.sin_f32(x)`); the functions that do not already exist as methods in std.core.float are also inherent
// methods of f32 and f64 (`x.sin()`, `y.atan2(x)`), available when std.math is part of the program. Constants:
// `std.math.PI` (f64, re-exported from std.math.f64.consts) and `std.math.f32.consts.PI` (f32, after `use std.math.f32.consts`).
//
// Accuracy (checked against 300-bit mpmath references, see tests/std/math/README.md): every f64 function is within
// 0.52 ulp; the f32 functions evaluate the f64 function and round once, so they are within 0.5 ulp + 2^-29 ulp of
// f32. SPEC-41 asks for <= 1.0 ulp (<= 1.5 ulp for tan, powf, the hyperbolic functions and erf).
//
// Internals: std.math.dd (double-double arithmetic), std.math.tables (generated constants) and one module per
// family: exp_core, log_core, trig_core, atan_core, pow_core, hyper_core, erf_core.

use std.core.float.to_bits_f64
use std.core.float.from_bits_f64
use std.math.dd.scale2

